{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RWtRR5CodYgS6CAGXCwZ9x","type":"tool_result","content":"      5 \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3f76de56b20aa4546ca3e259259008c0a78bdbab63f7245bdeba7fde17663b4c\"\npaper.tex:95:\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension (below); the two have identical development scores, the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy, and the available evidence cannot say which ran. Statements about the ``evaluated pair'' therefore refer to this selector--imputer family. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\npaper.tex:244:Table~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration. % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\npaper.tex:248:\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair, read from its source copy (not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree).}\npaper.tex:271:The longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1040cdf-154d-4e61-b013-8e99c7905ab4","uuid":"00fb5468-112f-4595-b34e-3ee58bd685fe","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:14:34.596Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"      5 \"program_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3f76de56b20aa4546ca3e259259008c0a78bdbab63f7245bdeba7fde17663b4c\"\npaper.tex:95:\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension (below); the two have identical development scores, the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy, and the available evidence cannot say which ran. Statements about the ``evaluated pair'' therefore refer to this selector--imputer family. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\npaper.tex:244:Table~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration. % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\npaper.tex:248:\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair, read from its source copy (not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree).}\npaper.tex:271:The longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
