AUDIT_SUMMARY: The numbers match the evaluator outputs and the grounded claims. Four wording issues remain: ungrounded BenchBench and redundancy statements, an ungrounded missingness claim with a wrong scale description, an unsupported sealed-run identity claim, and a hyperparameter table whose source is not tied to the sealed run.
AUDIT_FINDING_COUNT: 4
AUDIT_FINDING: id=benchbench-redundancy-ungrounded || section=Abstract and Related Work (Benchmark suites and their overlap) || claim=Related Work: "Perlitz2024BenchBench show that conclusions about benchmark agreement depend strongly on methodological choices such as the reference set and the correlation measure; we therefore define the target, the held-out split and the aggregation explicitly". Abstract: "historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant". || issue=Only titles and metadata of the cited papers were retained, with no abstracts or snippets. The grounded claim says only that agreement analyses "bear on how related benchmark scores are". The specific BenchBench finding (reference set, correlation measure, strong dependence) and the "strongly redundant" premise have no support. The previous audit round already asked for this level of detail to be removed, and it is still in the Related Work paragraph and the abstract. || evidence=references_provenance.json entry Perlitz2024BenchBench (title and venue only); claims_grounding.json claim "Agreement analyses across benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models bear on how related benchmark scores are"; paper.tex Related Work paragraph 1 and Abstract sentence 1. The Introduction already uses the softer "plausibly overlapping". || fix=Reduce the BenchBench sentence to "agreement analyses across benchmarks \citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench} bear on how related benchmark scores are", and drop the "therefore" motivation clause. Change the abstract to "benchmarks may be substantially redundant" or similar hedged wording.
AUDIT_FINDING: id=scope-missingness-scales-unsupported || section=Discussion and Limitations (Scope, Threats to validity 3, Practical reading) || claim="Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks)"; "Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency"; "scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1)"; "a handful of well-covered benchmarks ... already remove a large part of the column-mean error" || issue=No missingness analysis against model strength or recency is in claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json or analysis/*.json, so these are stated as facts without evidence. The scale list is also wrong. The HELM mean-win-rate column is excluded, and the retained HELM columns are F1, EM, equivalent-CoT and BLEU-4. The Open LLM Leaderboard scores are raw 0-1 values. The Practical reading adds "well-covered" selection, which the evidence does not test, and the measured k grids are 1-20 depending on the leaderboard. || evidence=source_evidence/manifests/helm_final_manifest.json (score_definition: "Mean win rate column excluded"; the columns are F1, EM, Equivalent (CoT), BLEU-4); analysis/protocol_facts.json (no missingness statistics); claims_grounding.json has no missingness claim; paper.tex Discussion "Scope" and "Threats to validity" (3). || fix=Reword the missingness points as a possible concern ("if missingness correlates with model strength, the missing-at-random assumption may fail; we did not test this"). Replace the scale list with "accuracy-type, F1, exact-match and BLEU-type metrics". Remove "well-covered" from the Practical reading, or label it as an untested conjecture.
AUDIT_FINDING: id=sealed-identity-either-or-ungrounded || section=Abstract, Method (Compared approaches), Results (configuration comparison), Conclusion || claim="the sealed run used one member of this family (the described pair or its latent-skill extension)"; "The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension"; "the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy" || issue=The evidence trail records only the final program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...). It carries no hash for any search configuration, so it cannot be compared with the retained source copies. It also does not show that the sealed program was one of exactly two configurations. The "either/or" restriction is an inference from "best-scoring on development", and nothing in the artifacts records the selection. The hash does not match the simple pair either. The sealed tree could be a different or modified tree. Grounding claim 8 cites only final.evidence.episodes and final.qualification.outcome, which do not support the restriction or the "matches neither" statement. || evidence=paper_data.json: all five final-evaluation entries carry the same program_tree_sha256, and the top_programs and all_programs entries carry none. claims_grounding.json claim 8 source_ids (final.evidence.episodes, final.qualification.outcome). The Limitations section states that "no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used". There is no hash-comparison artifact in source_evidence/. || fix=State that the identity of the sealed-evaluated source tree is not established. Say that the configuration was presumably the development-selected pair or its extension, but this is not recorded. Remove "matches neither retained source copy" unless a comparison artifact is cited, and apply the same hedged wording in the abstract and conclusion.
AUDIT_FINDING: id=si-hyperparameters-not-sealed-config || section=Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters) and Method (Selector and imputer) || claim="Cost and applicability ... the sealed evaluation of 14 episodes per seed took about 157--161 seconds"; "Several steps are guarded by wall-clock budgets (candidate evaluation after 70 s, full tuning after 150 s, stacking after 210 s), so on slow hardware the method degrades to the Gaussian-only model" || issue=The constants and the 70/150/210 s guards come from the proposed-pair source copy, which the paper says is not verified as the sealed tree. The Method text presents the guards and the degradation behavior as properties of the evaluated method without that caveat. The program source also shows that the 150 s guard switches to a quick tuning grid rather than the Gaussian-only model. Only the 210 s stacking guard falls back to the Gaussian-only model. || evidence=source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py lines 60-62 and 733-738 (FULL_TUNE_BUDGET leads to QUICK_SHRINKS, QUICK_RANKS and QUICK_LAMS; STACK_BUDGET leads to the Gaussian-only model); paper.tex Method "Cost and applicability" versus the SI Table hyper caption. || fix=Qualify the Cost and applicability sentences as properties of the proposed-pair source copy. Describe the guards accurately: optional candidates are skipped after 70 s, tuning falls back to a smaller grid after 150 s, and stacking is skipped (Gaussian-only) after 210 s.
